**Theorem 7.1 (pointwise total minimum).** In the full original class
of Definition 1.2,

$$
\boxed{
N(s)=\begin{cases}
6,&0\leq s\leq s_\dagger,\\
6,&s\in\mathcal E_{\rm abs},\\
7,&s_\dagger<s\leq s_0,\quad s\notin\mathcal E_{\rm abs},\\
8,&s_0<s\leq1.
\end{cases}}
\tag{TA.39}
$$

In particular $N(0)=N(s_\dagger)=6$, $N(s_0)=7$, and $N(1)=8$.
All minima are attained by common full-output instruments of §§8–9.

Proof of the weak lower bounds. The credited actual-low-label theorem
requires three low live-bearing labels through contact and four above
it. Height equality in TA.17 places their coefficient rays exactly on
the credited three plateau/initial rays or four positive-mass RC rays.
Additional labels can subdivide these masses but cannot introduce a
different optimal ray. The strictly positive high mass
$(1-\delta)A_{27}$ requires at least one actual high label. Every
such label has zero supported later failure by sharp cost.

Lemma 3.1 requires both failure spectral signs to occur and forbids
their coexistence at one actual label. Through contact neither may
share a live label, so there are at least $3+1+2=6$ outcomes.
Above contact negative failure still requires its own failure-only
outcome. Outside TA.38 positive failure also requires its own, giving
$4+1+2=7$. On TA.38 the unconditional lower bound is $4+1+1=6$.
One cannot evade these bounds by extra low labels: their rays are
still the same and each has one common decoder governed by TA.18.
A failure-only label of dimension at least three cannot carry
supported failure, since it would contradict the same sharp-cost
equality. Complement-only outcomes can add labels but cannot remove
any of the necessary supported outcomes. At $s=s_0$, Lemma 6.3
forces seven.

Proof of the strong lower bound. First the real-null argument of
Lemma 3.2 assigns one ray to each actual low live-bearing label. JR's
strong equality fixes four distinct positive ray masses, so four
actual low labels are necessary. This step uses the common decoder,
not coefficient rank alone. Sharp equality forbids supported high
later failure; Lemma 6.3 forces both spectral signs into two further
failure-only labels.

It remains to prove that the two high directions need two **actual**
labels. Orient the winding sign positively. If $m_\gamma\in[-1,1]$
is an actual label's mean binary report, then the sharp-cost proof
has the chain

$$
s\leq\mathsf V(F)\leq\sum_j|x_j||z_j|
 \leq\sqrt{u_sv_s}+\sqrt{(1-u_s)(1-v_s)}=s.
\tag{TA.40}
$$

The products have coefficients $(x_j,z_j,w_j)$ in order $(D,T,S)$;
the two Cauchy–Schwarz sums partition them by actual low/high fibre
membership. The first upper bound includes
$|m_\gamma\operatorname{Re}(\overline{x_j}z_j)|\leq|x_j||z_j|$
for products in that label. At equality every nonzero high product has

$$
|m_\gamma|=1,\qquad
z_j=m_\gamma q_sx_j,\qquad
q_s=\sqrt{\frac{1-v_s}{1-u_s}}>0.
\tag{TA.41}
$$

Cauchy–Schwarz equality fixes the common ratio; a high product with
$x_j=0$ must be zero. Its phases align by equality in the real-part
bound, and its actual mean report is deterministic. These statements
hold for each product despite arbitrary inaccessible multiplicity.
The high Gram has total $|x|^2$ mass $1-u_s>0$ and zero cross moment,
so

$$
\sum_{m_\gamma=+1,j}|x_j|^2
 =\sum_{m_\gamma=-1,j}|x_j|^2=(1-u_s)/2>0.
\tag{TA.42}
$$

Both report signs must therefore occur on actual high labels. A
single label with a common report kernel cannot supply both, even
with a larger fibre or many inaccessible Kraus matrices. Thus the
total is at least $4+2+2=8$. At $s=s_0$ the high $T$ coefficient
is zero; TA.41 has $q_s=0$ and does not force two reports. This is
why that endpoint can have seven labels.

These lower proofs start with an arbitrary original full-output
optimizer and use only its supported equality consequences. Differing
full-channel representatives or failure-time measures cannot evade
them. A countable or admitted integral instrument with a purported
smaller finite total alphabet has finitely many nonzero CP-mass
outcomes, to which the same proofs apply. Positive nonatomic acquired
mass already has infinite alphabet; unacquired integral indices remain
inaccessible multiplicity. This proves the entire claimed necessity
scope. The matching upper bounds follow below. $\square$

